## DESCRIPTION
## Determine how the center and spread of a sampling distribution changes for sampling
## distributions with different sample sizes.
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(Statistics)
## DBchapter(Sampling distributions)
## DBsection(Sample mean)
## Institution(Fitchburg State University)
## Author(Jessie Oehrlein)
## Level(1)
## MO(1)
## KEYWORDS('statistic', 'parameter', 'sampling distribution', 'sample')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'parserPopUp.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context('Numeric');
$showPartialCorrectAnswers = 0;

$n = random(100, 300, 10);
$m = $n + 50;

@opts = ('greater than', 'equal to', 'less than', 'not enough information');
$dd1  = DropDown([@opts], 1);
$dd2  = DropDown([@opts], 2);

BEGIN_PGML

Across all US adults, the proportion of people who pay for a music streaming service is [` p `].  A study will ask a sample of adults whether they pay for a music streaming service.

a)    The center of the sampling distribution is [_]{$dd1} the population proportion [` p `]. 

b)   The spread of the sampling distribution for samples of size [$m] is [_]{$dd2} the spread of the sampling distribution for samples of size [$n]. 
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
a) For the distribution of sampling proportions, the center is always *equal to* the population proportion. 
b) The spread of the sample proportion is inversely proportional to the square root of the size of the sample. Therefore a larger sample size has a smaller spread or standard deviation than that of a smaller sample size, so the answer is *less than*.
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();  